A
smooth path $\rho:[0,1]\rightarrow\mathbb R^n$ in a scattering diagram $\mathfrak D$  is called {\bf finite transverse} if
\begin{itemize}
\item $\rho(0)$ and $\rho(1)$ are not in any walls;
\item whenever the image of $\rho$ intersects a wall $({\bf v},W)$, it crosses $W$ transversely;
\item the image of $\rho$ intersects finitely many walls, and does not intersect the boundary
of a wall or the intersection of two walls which span different hyperplanes.
\end{itemize}

Supposed that $\mathcal A(\mathcal S)$ is a skew-symmetrizable cluster algebra of rank $n$ with principal coefficients at $t_0$ and $({ f x}_t,{ f x}_{t^\prime})$ is a $g$-pair along $I=\{1,\cdots,n\} ackslash \{k\}$. Let $G_t, G_t^{\prime}$ be the $G$-matrices of $({ f x}_t,{ f x}_{t^\prime})$ respectively, and denote by $R_{t}^{t^\prime}=(r_{jl}):=G_{t^{\prime}}^{-1}G_t$. Let $d^{t^\prime}(x_{i;t})=(d_{1}^\prime,\cdots,d_{n}^\prime)$ be the $d$-vector of $x_{i;t}$ with respect to ${ f x}_{t^\prime}$. We have that (i). The $j$th row vector of $R_{t}^{t^\prime}$ is a non-negative vector for any $j
eq k$. (ii). $r_{ki}>0$ if and only if $d_{k}^\prime=-1$, and if only if $x_{i;t}\in{ f x}_{t^\prime}$ and $x_{i;t}=x_{k;t^\prime}$. (iii). $r_{ki}=0$ if and only if $d_{k}^\prime=0$, and if only if $x_{i;t}\in{ f x}_{t^\prime}$ and $x_{i;t}
eq x_{k;t^\prime}$. (iv). $r_{ki}<0$ if and only if $d_{k}^\prime>0$, and if only if $x_{i;t}
otin{ f x}_{t^\prime}$. In particular, $d_{k}^\prime=-1,0$ or $d_{k}^\prime>0$.

Let $\mathcal A(\mathcal S)$ is a skew-symmetrizable cluster algebra, and $d^{t_0}(x_{i;t}) = (d_1,\cdots,d_n)^{	op}$ be the $d$-vector of the cluster variable $x_{i;t}$ with respect to the cluster ${ f x}_{t_0}$ of $\mathcal A(\mathcal S)$. Then (i). $d_k$ depends only on $x_{i;t}$ and $x_{k;t_0}$, not on the clusters containing $x_{k;t_0}$, where $k=1,\cdots,n$; (ii). $d_k\geq -1$ for $k=1,\cdots,n$, and in details,  egin{eqnarray} d_k= egin{cases} -1~,& 	ext{iff}\;\; x_{i,t}=x_{k,t_0};\ 0~,& 	ext{iff}\;\;x_{i,t}
ot=x_{k,t_0}\;\;	ext{and}\;\; x_{i,t}, x_{k,t_0}\in { f x}_{t^\prime} \; 	ext{for some}\; t^\prime;\ 	ext{a positive integer}~, & 	ext{iff}\;\; 	ext{there exists no cluster } { f x}_{t^\prime} 	ext{ containing both }x_{i,t} 	ext{ and }x_{k,t_0} .\end{cases}
onumber \end{eqnarray} In particular, if $x_{i;t}
otin{ f x}_{t_0}$, then $d^{t_0}(x_{i;t})\in\mathbb N^n$, that is, the positivity of $d$-vectors holds.

(i). Let ${ f x}_{t^\prime}$ be another cluster of $\mathcal A(\mathcal S)$ containing $x_{k;t_0}$, and $d^{t^\prime}(x_{i;t})=(d_1^\prime,\cdots,d_n^\prime)^{	op}$ be the $d$-vector of $x_{i;t}$ with respect to ${ f x}_{t^\prime}$. Without loss of generality, we can assume that $x_{k;t^\prime}=x_{k;t_0}$. By Theorem ef{corconnected}, the cluster ${ f x}_{t^\prime}$ is connected with ${ f x}_{t_0}$ by a $\{1,2,\cdots,n\}\setminus\{k\}$-sequence. Then by Proposition ef{prodvectors}, we get that $d_k^\prime=d_k$. (ii). $d_k\geq -1$ follows from Corollary ef{thmdvectors} directly. The important thing is when $d_k$ takes values at $-1,0$, a positive integer. By Proposition ef{prodrec}, we know that the notion of $d$-vectors is independent of the choice of coefficient system. Thanks to Proposition ef{proclustervariable}, we can assume that $\mathcal A(\mathcal S)$ is a skew-symmetrizable cluster algebra with principal coefficients at $t_0$. (a). If $x_{i;t}=x_{k;t_0}$, clearly, $d_k=-1$. Conversely, if $d_k=-1$, then $x_{i;t}=x_{k;t_0}$ follows from Corollary ef{thmdvectors} (i). (b). By Corollary ef{thmdvectors}, if $d_k=0$, then there exists a cluster ${ f x}_{t^{\prime}}$ containing both $x_{i;t}$ and $x_{k;t_0}$. Since $d_k=0$, we must have $x_{i;t}
eq x_{k;t_0}$. Conversely, suppose that $x_{i;t}
eq x_{k;t_0}$ and there exists a cluster ${ f x}_{t^{\prime}}$ containing both $x_{i;t}$ and $x_{k;t_0}$. Without loss of generality, we can assume that $x_{i;t}=x_{1;t^\prime}$ and $x_{k;t_0}=x_{2;t^\prime}$. By (i), we know that $d_k$ does not depend on the cluster containing $x_{k;t_0}=x_{2;t^\prime}$, so $d_k$ is equal to the $2$-th component of $d^{t^\prime}(x_{i;t})$. Since $d^{t^\prime}(x_{i;t})=d^{t^\prime}(x_{1;t^{\prime}})=-{ f e}_1$, we get $d_k=0$. (c). By (a) and (b), $d_k\leq 0$ if and only if there exists a cluster containing both $x_{i,t}$ and $x_{k,t_0}$. So $d_k>0$ if and only if there exists no cluster containing both $x_{i,t}$ and $x_{k,t_0}$. (d). We have proved that $d_k\geq -1$, and if $d_k=-1$, then $x_{i;t}\in { f x}_{t_0}$. So if $x_{i;t}
otin{ f x}_{t_0}$, then each $d_k$ is nonnegative, and we thus $d^{t_0}(x_{i;t})\in\mathbb N^n$. The proof is finished.

