otin { f x}_{t_0}$. Then by $a_k>0$ and $(d_{k1}^{t_0}v_1+\cdots+d_{kn}^{t_0}v_n)+a_k\leq0$, we can obtain ${ f v}
otin \mathbb N^n, { f v}\in V$, i.e., ${ f x}_t^{ f a}$ is a sum of proper Laurent monomials in ${ f x}_{t_0}$. So the proper Laurent monomial property holds for $\mathcal A(\mathcal S)$ and thus the cluster monomials of $\mathcal A(\mathcal S)$ are linear independence.

Thanks to Theorem \ref{thmfg} (ii), we have that
\begin{eqnarray}
{\bf x}_{t_0}^{g(x_{j;t^\prime})}=x_{j;t^{\prime}}|_{y_1=\cdots=y_n=0}=\sum\limits_{p\in P}\lambda_{p,j}{\bf x}_{t}^{{\bf r}_{p,j;t}^{t^\prime}}|_{y_1=\cdots=y_n=0}=
\sum\limits_{p\in P}\lambda_{p,j}{\bf x}_{t_0}^{G_t{\bf r}_{p,j;t}^{t^\prime}}, \lambda_{p,j}> 0.\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
The above equality holds if and only if $P$ has exactly one element (say $p_0$), and
\begin{eqnarray} \lambda_{p_0,j}=1,\; g(x_{j;t^\prime})=G_t{\bf r}_{p_0,j;t}^{t^\prime}.\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}

By Theorem \ref{detgthm} (i),  the Laurent expansion of the cluster monomial  ${\bf x}_{t_1}^{\bf a}$ with respect to ${\bf x}_{t_2}$  has the following form
\begin{equation} {\bf x}_{t_1}^{\bf a}={\bf x}_{t_2}^{{\bf b}}(1+\sum\limits_{0\neq {\bf v}\in\mathbb N^n,~~{\bf u}\in\mathbb Z^n}c_{\bf v}{\bf y}^{\bf v}{\bf x}_{t_2}^{\bf u}),\;\;c_{\bf v}\geq 0.
\end{equation}

Then by (\ref{equ12}),  ${\bf x}_{t_1}^{\bf a}$ can be written as follows:
\begin{eqnarray} {\bf x}_{t_1}^{\bf a}={\bf x}_{t_2}^{{\bf f}}+\sum\limits_{0\neq {\bf v_1}\in\mathbb N^n,~~{\bf u_1}\in\mathbb Z^n}c_{\bf v_1}{\bf y}^{\bf v_1}{\bf x}_{t_2}^{\bf u_1},\;\;\text{with}\;\; c_{\bf v_1}\geq 0.
\end{eqnarray}

Replacing $f_1,\cdots, f_n$ into $x_{1;t}={\bf x}_{t^\prime}^{\bf v}$, we obtain that
  \begin{eqnarray} x_{1;t}=f_{1}^{v_1}\cdots f_{n}^{v_n}.
 \end{eqnarray}

Because $\pi_I(g(x_{k;t}))=\pi_I(g({\bf x}_{t^{\prime}}^{{\bf v}}))=\pi_I(G_{t^{\prime}}{\bf v})$, we can assume that
\begin{eqnarray}
g(x_{k;t})&=&(g_1,\cdots,g_p,g_{p+1},\cdots,g_n)^{\top};\nonumber\\
g({\bf x}_{t^{\prime}}^{{\bf v}})&=&G_{t^{\prime}}{\bf v}=(g_1,\cdots,g_p,g_{p+1}^{\prime},\cdots,g_n^{\prime})^{\top},\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
where $g_i,g_i^{\prime}\geq 0$ for $i=p+1,\cdots,n$. Then it is easy to see that $x_{k;t}\prod\limits_{i=p+1}^nx_{i;t}^{g_i^{\prime}}$ and ${\bf x}_{t^{\prime}}^{{\bf v}}\prod\limits_{i=p+1}^nx_{i;t^{\prime}}^{g_i}$ are cluster monomials in ${\bf x}_t$ and in ${\bf x}_{t^{\prime}}$ respectively and
\begin{eqnarray}
g(x_{k;t}\prod\limits_{i=p+1}^nx_{i;t}^{g_i^{\prime}})=(g_1,\cdots,g_p,g_{p+1}+g_{p+1}^{\prime},
\cdots,g_{n}+g_{n}^{\prime})^{\top}=g({\bf x}_{t^{\prime}}^{{\bf v}}\prod\limits_{i=p+1}^nx_{i;t^{\prime}}^{g_i}).\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
